    sha256: e54a2eb89b7e4d4d4ff30802ef129a0ea6c418e6ece7a4e70d142dcb5a7de18b
  body: "# Hardship as privation of basic necessities\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe captured JSA
    hardship chapter asks whether hardship would occur if JSA were unpaid and describes severe suffering
    or privation of basic necessities, with all relevant circumstances considered.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\
    \nDMG 35000 excludes IS from this chapter’s hardship provisions; DMG 35001 concerns specified JSA(IB)
    payments. Access conditions, periods, income and other-benefit exclusions remain separate. Hardship
    is not established by a vulnerability label alone, and no payment or award is calculated.\n\n## Inspected
    source passages\n\n- [DMG 35000, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6895c9563080e72710b2e2c6/dmg-ch-35.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 35: Hardship.\n- [DMG 35001, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6895c9563080e72710b2e2c6/dmg-ch-35.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 35: Hardship.\n- [DMG 35153, PDF page 39](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6895c9563080e72710b2e2c6/dmg-ch-35.pdf#page=39)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 35: Hardship.\n- [DMG 35155, PDF page 39](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6895c9563080e72710b2e2c6/dmg-ch-35.pdf#page=39)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 35: Hardship.\n- [DMG 35156, PDF page 39](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6895c9563080e72710b2e2c6/dmg-ch-35.pdf#page=39)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 35: Hardship.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation of
    selected passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review is required.
    Source acquisition, source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring time are separate.\n\
    \nThis is an independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document, individual advice,
    an entitlement decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter reading order, footnotes
    and characters. No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural evaluation pass is
    claimed by this record.\n\n- `dmg-vol6-ch35`: PDF SHA-256 `e54a2eb89b7e4d4d4ff30802ef129a0ea6c418e6ece7a4e70d142dcb5a7de18b`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `f636b425bb3f5b43a4d10c90c7d93e6a5e1abecd710a1d751b97704b24345753`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/legacy-jsa-hardship-vulnerable-group
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The chapter explicitly distinguishes the hardship test from its specialised
      vulnerable-group categories used for access without a waiting period.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol6-ch35/0013
      quote: "35055 [See memo DMG 18/17] Vulnerable group is the term used in this guidance to describe
        people\nwho are more likely to suffer hardship if JSA is not paid without a waiting period, i.e.
        those people who are\n\ndefined as “persons in hardship” in JSA legislation1. Claimants in a defined
        vulnerable group are entitled\n\nto hardship payments in circumstances where other claimants are
        not 2.\n\n\nNote 1: The test of hardship is not a test of ‘vulnerability’ but a test of the lack
        of the basic essential\nnecessities of life, e.g. accommodation, food, water, heating and hygiene
        (see DMG 35155).\n\n\nNote 2: The term ‘vulnerable group’ for hardship purposes is not the same
        as the DWP wider definition of\n‘vulnerability’, or ‘vulnerable groups’ for any other purposes
        and applies exclusively to those defined in\n\nJSA regulations as “persons in hardship”1. Where
        the term ‘vulnerable group’ is used throughout this\nchapter it means those defined exclusively
        for hardship purposes (i.e. those who have access to hardship\nwithout a waiting period).\n\n\n\
        \                                                      1 JSA Regs, regs 140(1) & 146A(1); 2 regs
        141 & 146C(1)"
      locator: DMG 35055
  - target: term/legacy-jsa-hardship-available-resources
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: Assessment of available resources is one specified part of the hardship enquiry, with
      express limits on what can reasonably be expected.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol6-ch35/0044
      quote: "35175 Resources is not defined in the law. The word should be given its normal everyday
        meaning 1 of\nthe means available or a stock or supply that can be drawn upon.\n\n\n         \
        \                                                                                       1 R(SB)
        19/82"
      locator: DMG 35175
    - page: page/dmg-vol6-ch35/0045
      quote: "35177 It is not considered reasonable to expect claimants to rely upon\n\n\n1. charities,
        such as food banks, (see DMG 35214)\n\n\n2. increase debts by seeking credit or using or extending
        overdraft facilities (see DMG 35196)\n\n\n3. sell or pawn items to obtain cash (see DMG 35213)\n\
        \n\n4. find cheaper housing (see DMG 35216) or\n\n\n5. ask friends and family for help (see DMG
        35217).\n\n\nThese are not considered available resources. While claimants may be prepared to
        request such help\nthere is no requirement to do so and claimants should not be denied access
        to hardship payments if they\ndon’t."
      locator: DMG 35177
